# `ptr` — a header field's ADDRESS crosses to C (panel 164; design.md §4.19,
# §4.10).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT A ROW IN `emit/builtins.hero`. That module
# answers with a C entry point, and this built-in becomes no call at all: it is
# one cast written at the use site. `emit/bytes_text.hero` is the sibling from
# the sitting before, and it is the OTHER direction — that one COPIES the bytes
# into a `str` the runtime terminates, this one lends the field where it lies.
#
# WHY THE OPERAND IS RENDERED AND NOT READ FROM `arguments[0]`. A fixed array has
# no C storage to be a temporary in — `emit/ctype.hero` answers `C has no
# assignable array` for it on purpose — so the prologue declares nothing and the
# argument names an identifier that does not exist. `emit/storageless.hero` is
# the renderer for exactly this class, and panel 162's implementation found the
# trap the hard way: `use of undeclared identifier 't1300'` from clang.
#
# WHAT THE CAST IS, AND WHY IT IS `void *` AND NOT `const void *`. C writes
# through this pointer as well as reading through it, which is the half of the
# boundary panel 164 found had no door at all: of 141 pointer parameters across
# 16 real headers, **50 are non-`const`** — `getcwd`, `read`, `uuid_generate` —
# and nothing in this language could produce their argument. A `const` cast would
# close the direction the sitting was convened to open.
#
# AND WHY NEVER `const char *`. That is `cstr`'s spelling and `cstr` is a promise
# of a terminating zero that a header's `char[N]` field does not make: 37 of 50
# such fields across those headers have none. design.md §1.11 lists that promise
# among the decisions not to revisit. The text route stays `validated_bytes`,
# which copies.

use check/state
use ir
use ir/containers
use emit/writer
use emit/aggregate
use ast
use source

# The C text of the PLACE a lent field lives in — `h0_s.nsap`, never `t13.nsap`.
#
# THIS IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS OF THE LEND AND IT WAS MEASURED WRONG FIRST.
# The first draft rendered with `emit/storageless.hero`'s `fixed_text`, which is
# right for `validated_bytes` and wrong here: the IR loads a record into a fresh
# temporary before reading a field of it, so `fixed_text` gave `(void *)(t13.nsap)`
# — the address of a COPY. Reading through it is correct by accident, because the
# copy holds the same bytes. Writing through it is lost in silence: the probe
# filled eight bytes, C returned, and the program's record was unchanged, at exit
# 0 with every suite green. A silently useless write is the class this language
# exists to refuse, so the renderer follows the load back to the cell instead.
#
# It walks producers rather than trusting one shape: a `.load` is the cell and a
# `.field` is one step off it, so `s.inner.nsap` resolves as far as it needs to.
# Anything else is not a place and fails, which `check/lending.hero` has already
# refused at the source — this is the second witness, not the first.
function lent_place(c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: containers.Function, value: i64) -> str?
    for blk in f.blocks
        for inst in blk.insts
            if !inst.dest.is_err() && inst.dest.must() == value
                match inst.op
                    .load ld => return ok(aggregate.place(c, s, decls, f, at: ld.place))
                    .field fl =>
                        owner = containers.value_type(f, fl.base)
                        found = aggregate.field(c, s, decls, owner: owner, index: fl.at)

                        if found.is_err()
                            return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a field of this type")
                        base = lent_place(c, s, decls, f, value: fl.base)?
                        return ok(base + "." + found.must().member)
                    .lit | .store | .push_owned | .unary | .binary | .cast => return fail(
                        code: "none",
                        msg: "not a place"
                    )
                    .call | .construct | .index | .map_get | .len_of => return fail(
                        code: "none",
                        msg: "not a place"
                    )
                    .tag_of | .payload | .func_ref | .abort_op | .incref => return fail(
                        code: "none",
                        msg: "not a place"
                    )
                    .decref | .decref_slot | .hole | .missing | .copy_out => return fail(
                        code: "none",
                        msg: "not a place"
                    )

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no producer")

# Emit `into = ptr(value)`.
function lent(@w: writer.Writer, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], f: containers.Function, run: ir.Run, into: str)
    given = containers.args_of(f, run)
    source_value: i64 @ 0 - 1

    if given.len() > 0
        match given[0]
            .value_arg v => source_value @ v.value
            .inout_arg   => source_value @ source_value

    if source_value < 0
        writer.line(@w, text: "    hero_unreachable(); /* ptr lost its argument */")
        return
    place = lent_place(c, s, decls, f, value: source_value)

    # The checker admitted only a field of a named binding here, so a miss means
    # the two passes disagree and it is loud rather than a guess
    # (`emit/convert.hero`'s rule, one boundary over).
    if place.is_err()
        writer.line(@w, text: "    hero_unreachable(); /* a lent field with no place */")
        return
    writer.line(@w, text: "    " + into + " = (void *)(" + place.must() + ");")
